                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-subscription-arrives-with-no-cell-to-handle-it
     :why "the top-level triggers block is what a relay reads to decide what to deliver, so a collection listed there with no cell is a silent drop rather than an error. oilRefining.refinery arrives from a repo this one does not control"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilRefining.refinery\""
     :replace "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilRefining.crude\""
     :must-fail ["every-subscribed-collection-has-a-cell-that-can-handle-it"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-pipeline-is-triggered-by-a-collection-nobody-advertises
     :why "this manifest has two subscription surfaces that are allowed to differ in size -- the top-level block advertises five, the pipeline list runs steps for one. A pipeline keyed to a collection outside the advertised set never fires, and nothing about it looks wrong. The replacement here is a collection that DOES have a cell, so the coverage check stays green and only the subset check moves"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilMidstream.flow\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"subscribeRepos\""
     :replace "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.standard.shinkaEvolution\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"subscribeRepos\""
     :must-fail ["the-pipeline-subscriptions-are-a-subset-of-the-advertised-ones"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-required-loop-loses-the-cell-that-runs-it
     :why "requiredLoops is an obligation the standard rule places on this actor, and a loop with no cell simply never runs -- there is no error, the loop is just absent. Nothing in the repo connected the two before this suite"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\n    \"koji\",\n"
     :replace "\n    \"koji-v2\",\n"
     :must-fail ["every-required-loop-and-collection-has-a-cell"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/custom-escape-hatch-allowed
     :why "a step whose fn is outside the declared capability vocabulary runs with authority the manifest never granted. `custom` is the specific escape hatch the retired vitest file was written to refuse, so losing it would undo the one check that file was carried over for"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"fn\": \"derive:social\""
     :replace "\"fn\": \"custom\""
     :must-fail ["no-pipeline-step-escapes-into-a-custom-function"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-step-reaches-for-a-capability-the-actor-never-declared
     :why "the vitest file only checked steps against the global vocabulary, so a step could use any known capability whether or not THIS actor declared it. graph.vectorSearch is in the vocabulary and not in this manifest's capabilities list -- the check that catches it is the per-actor one, and only that one moves here"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"fn\": \"graph.write\""
     :replace "\"fn\": \"graph.vectorSearch\""
     :must-fail ["every-pipeline-step-fn-is-a-capability-the-manifest-declared"]}

    {:id :oilup/served-did-document-is-reconciled
     :why "hazard tripwire. .well-known/did.json identifies itself as did:web:etzhayyim.com:actor:oil-upstream while the planner and the manifest both stamp did:web:oil-upstream.etzhayyim.com, and alsoKnownAs bridges neither. Which side moves is an ownership decision; this fires whichever side moves first"
     :file ".well-known/did.json"
     :find "\"id\" : \"did:web:etzhayyim.com:actor:oil-upstream\","
     :replace "\"id\" : \"did:web:oil-upstream.etzhayyim.com\","
     :must-fail ["hazard-the-served-did-document-does-not-claim-the-did-this-actor-stamps"]}

    {:id :oilup/the-evidence-floor-itself
     :why "the floor under the suite, guarded like anything else. Dropping a namespace from the runner leaves a run that prints `0 failures, 0 errors` and would otherwise be read as a pass -- the exact shape ADR-2608136000 is about. It must exit 2, not 0 and not 1, and it must not print the marker. `must-fail` names the REFUSED line rather than a test, because the point is that no test ran"
     :file "run_tests.cljs"
     :find "(t/run-tests 'oil_upstream.murakumo-test\n             'oil_upstream.manifest-test)"
     :replace "(t/run-tests 'oil_upstream.manifest-test)"
     :must-fail ["REFUSED"]}]}
  {:repo "orgs/cloud-itonami/gov-municipality"
   :label "gov-municipality permitting actor: the gate, the clock, and the six files that describe one actor"
   ;; Until 2026-08-31 this repo's suite was green exactly one way: `run_tests.sh`
   ;; built a mktemp symlink farm to reconcile namespaces with paths that did not
   ;; match them, and drove four namespaces through `bb` -- a retired script host
   ;; here (ADR-2607173000), reached through a .sh file, which is not an authoring
   ;; surface either. `methods/agent.cljc` was .cljc in extension only: it read
   ;; `System/currentTimeMillis` and referred to `clojure.string/split` without
   ;; requiring it, so Babashka's preload was the only thing holding it up.
   ;; `src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc` -- the fail-closed gate -- was in none
   ;; of the four namespaces and had never been executed on any runtime.
   ;;
   ;; All ten below were replayed by hand at 9c5827be on 2026-08-31, one at a
   ;; time with the file restored between runs, before any of them was written
   ;; here. Every anchor was checked to occur exactly once; base green before and
   ;; after; no mutation printed the green marker. The runner's three exit codes
   ;; were each shown to discriminate rather than assumed: breaking
   ;; `missing-gates` gives exit 1 with 28 failures and no marker, dropping a
   ;; namespace from `run-tests` gives exit 2 while printing "0 failures,
   ;; 0 errors", and the unmodified tree gives exit 0 with the marker. The last
   ;; is registered below, so the floor is guarded the same way the code is.
   :cmd ["nbb" "--classpath" "src:test" "run_tests.cljs"]
   :green-marker "gov-municipality: all green"
   :mutations
   [{:id :gov/gate-check-removed
     :why "the invariant the boundary exists for. With nothing removed from :required-gates, `missing-gates` returns empty for every input and every cell plans as :ready -- an actor that writes to the MST without a single attestation. This is the shape the drift takes if the gate is ever 'simplified'"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(remove #(boolean (gate-value attestations %)))"
     :replace "(remove (fn [_] true))"
     :must-fail ["blocked-plan-emits-no-effects"]}

    {:id :gov/presence-counts-as-attestation
     :why "a gate recorded as `false` is a refusal, not a signature. `contains?` is the plausible-looking edit -- it reads as 'the attestation is present' -- and it turns every explicit `false` into a pass. Nothing else in the suite distinguishes an absent gate from one that was actively denied"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  (or (get attestations gate)"
     :replace "  (or (and (map? attestations) (contains? attestations gate))"
     :must-fail ["falsey-attestation-is-a-refusal-not-a-signature"]}

    {:id :gov/path-separator-survives-into-rkey
     :why "`safe-rkey` decides what a record is called in the MST. Widening the safe alphabet by one character lets a slash through, and an rkey containing a path separator is not a key -- it is a path. The DIDs this actor derives rkeys from are full of colons and dots, so the alphabet is load-bearing"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~-]\" \"-\")"
     :replace "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~/-]\" \"-\")"
     :must-fail ["safe-rkey-strips-did-web-and-unsafe-characters"]}

    {:id :gov/clock-goes-back-to-jvm-only
     :why "the exact regression this commit removed. `System/currentTimeMillis` in a .cljc file is invisible on the JVM -- it is the runtime it works on -- so only a run that is not the JVM can see it. nbb refuses at analysis, so :must-fail names the refusal rather than a test: the namespace never loads, and 'the suite went red' would otherwise count any unrelated breakage as this one"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/methods/agent.cljc"
     :find "#?(:clj (System/currentTimeMillis)\n     :cljs (.now js/Date))"
     :replace "(System/currentTimeMillis)"
     :must-fail ["Unable to resolve symbol: System/currentTimeMillis"]}

    {:id :gov/clojure-string-require-dropped
     :why "the other half of that regression. The file referred to `clojure.string/split` for months without requiring the namespace and nobody noticed, because Babashka preloads it. Dropping the require again is silent on bb and refused by nbb at the first `str/` call"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/methods/agent.cljc"
     :find "  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))"
     :replace "  )"
     :must-fail ["Unable to resolve symbol: str/starts-with?"]}

    {:id :gov/app-manifest-src-drifts
     :why "kotoba.app.edn names the three files `kotoba app deploy` compiles. It fell behind the tree during this very commit -- the cells moved and the manifest did not follow -- and the test caught it. This is that drift, replayed"
     :file "kotoba.app.edn"
     :find "\"src/gov_municipality/cells/final_sign_off/state_machine.cljc\""
     :replace "\"cells/final_sign_off/state_machine.cljc\""
     :must-fail ["kotoba-app-src-paths-exist"]}

    {:id :gov/cell-cites-an-undeclared-gate
     :why "a cell sidecar claiming to enforce a gate the actor does not declare. The gate ids are the actor's constitutional surface (manifest.edn :actor/gates); a cell citing G21 is either a typo or a gate somebody removed from the manifest without following it into the cells"
     :file "cells/permit_submission.edn"
     :find ":cell/gates [\"G1\" \"G15\" \"G16\" \"G19\" \"G20\"]"
     :replace ":cell/gates [\"G1\" \"G15\" \"G16\" \"G19\" \"G21\"]"
     :must-fail ["cells-only-cite-gates-the-manifest-declares"]}

    {:id :gov/lex-record-leaves-the-shared-root
     :why "the four lexicon records must agree on one root, whatever it is. This repo already spells its lexicon root three different ways across prose, cljc and lex/*.edn -- that divergence is pinned by a separate test. What must not happen on top of it is the lex files themselves splitting"
     :file "lex/inspectionSchedule.edn"
     :find ":lex/id \"com.etzhayyim.govmunicipality.inspectionSchedule\""
     :replace ":lex/id \"com.etzhayyim.gov.inspectionSchedule\""
     :must-fail ["lex-records-share-one-root-and-end-with-their-declared-name"]}

    {:id :gov/manifest-cell-loses-its-sidecar
     :why "manifest.edn and cells/*.edn are two lists of the same cells. Renaming one entry leaves a declared cell with no sidecar and a sidecar no manifest entry -- checked in both directions, because only one direction catches a rename"
     :file "manifest.edn"
     :find "{:cell/id \"ledger\"                :cell/kind :datalog   :cell/runtime :kotoba}"
     :replace "{:cell/id \"audit\"                 :cell/kind :datalog   :cell/runtime :kotoba}"
     :must-fail ["every-manifest-cell-has-a-sidecar-and-every-sidecar-a-manifest-entry"]}

    {:id :gov/namespace-dropped-from-the-runner
     :why "the floor, guarded like the code. A namespace quietly dropped from `run-tests` prints '0 failures, 0 errors' -- the same words a full green run prints -- so without the floor a suite that stopped measuring the boundary reads as a suite that found nothing wrong. It must exit 2, not 0 and not 1, and must not print the marker. :must-fail names the REFUSED line rather than a test, because the point is that the tests did not run"
     :file "run_tests.cljs"
     :find "             'gov_municipality.murakumo-test\n             'gov_municipality.manifest-test)"
     :replace "             'gov_municipality.manifest-test)"
     :must-fail ["REFUSED"]}]}
]}
